+// iOS: the host allocator passes blocks straight to malloc/free, without its header.
+// ReXGlue replaces operator new/delete in every binary; elsewhere the host executable's forwarders
+// make that replacement process-wide. On iOS that is not guaranteed: an app run inside a container
+// app (e.g. LiveContainer) is not the process's main executable, so the system C++ library keeps its
+// own operator new. On an iPad Pro M2 (2026-11-18) MoltenVK then freed a libc++-allocated block
+// through ReXGlue's operator delete and the header check aborted. With plain malloc blocks either
+// allocator can free any block. Cost on iOS only: no block checks and no per-tag statistics.
 
